(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is benzoyl peroxide added when a topical or oral antibiotic is used for acne?","choices":["It prevents the photosensitivity that tetracyclines can cause","It shortens the course of oral antibiotic therapy to two weeks","It decreases the development of antibiotic resistance","It increases the absorption of the antibiotic into the follicle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is also keratolytic and anti-inflammatory in its own right. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does a carbuncle differ from a furuncle at presentation?","choices":["It presents as a single opening with a surrounding zone of erythema","It presents as a plaque raised above the skin with a demarcated edge","It is extremely painful and more often produces malaise, chills and fever","It is painless and produces no systemic symptoms of any kind at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Systemic symptoms are more common with a carbuncle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Why should topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide not be applied at the same time?","choices":["Because tretinoin blocks the antimicrobial effect that benzoyl peroxide has","Because of the risk of skin irritation, so at least three hours should separate them","Because benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin, so alternate days are recommended","Because the combination raises the risk of photosensitivity in treated areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That interval is specified in the patient education. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does the 2016 American Academy of Dermatology guideline recommend for comedonal acne?","choices":["A topical antimicrobial with a topical retinoid, or benzoyl peroxide with an antibiotic","A topical retinoid with an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide together","Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy over a sixteen to twenty week treatment course","A topical retinoid, switching to azelaic or salicylic acid if it is not tolerated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the entry point of the treatment ladder. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A child with impetigo has lesions too numerous and widespread for topical mupirocin. Which oral options apply?","choices":["Doxycycline, minocycline or sarecycline for a three-month course","Dicloxacillin, amoxicillin-clavulanate, cephalexin, or clindamycin if penicillin allergic","Penicillin V, or else clindamycin if the patient reports a penicillin allergy","Ciprofloxacin, linezolid, or trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cephalexin is the drug of choice in children. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What isolation advice is given for a child with impetigo?","choices":["No isolation is needed at any point, since impetigo does not spread person to person","Isolate for a full ten days from the appearance of the very first crusted lesion","Isolate until treatment has been underway for twenty-four to forty-eight hours","Isolate until every crusted lesion has completely healed and the skin is clear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and do not share towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths or razors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How does a cutaneous abscess differ in origin from a furuncle?","choices":["An abscess arises from an infected hair follicle, whereas a furuncle follows traumatic inoculation of bacteria","An abscess arises from an obstructed apocrine gland, whereas a furuncle follows a break in the epidermis","An abscess arises from lymphatic spread of streptococci, whereas a furuncle spreads along the dermis","An abscess usually follows traumatic inoculation of bacteria, whereas a furuncle arises from an infected follicle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abscesses are often polymicrobial, with Staphylococcus aureus the commonest organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What must not be allowed to happen in suspected necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Laboratory tests and imaging must not delay surgical intervention","Broad-spectrum antibiotics must not be started before cultures return","The patient must not be admitted to a burn or trauma centre","A team approach must not delay the primary surgeon's decision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is a surgical emergency with high mortality. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"What is the natural course of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Progressive without oral ciprofloxacin in every patient","Chronic and relapsing for months after a single exposure","Resolving only after the affected follicles are drained","Usually self-limiting, clearing in two to ten days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ciprofloxacin is reserved for widespread or resistant cases. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How does acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis present after impetigo?","choices":["Painless jaundice with pale stools and progressive weight loss","Sudden oedema, haematuria with smoky or tea-coloured urine, proteinuria and hypertension","Migratory arthritis, a new heart murmur and subcutaneous nodules over the elbows","Fever with a sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue and circumoral pallor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — especially in three to seven year olds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which patient education is given for erythrasma?","choices":["Shower after any hot tub use and check the disinfectant level","Keep the area clean and dry, avoid excessive heat or moisture, and maintain a healthy body weight","Avoid sharing towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths and razors","Stop smoking and avoid any constrictive clothing over the affected area at all times"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — heat and humidity are the favourable conditions for the organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What should be suspected when acne scarring seems out of proportion to the severity of lesions?","choices":["An undiagnosed hyperandrogenic state driving deeper inflammation","Resistance of Cutibacterium acnes to the prescribed antimicrobial","An underlying immunodeficiency impairing the healing of each lesion","Patient manipulation of the lesions through picking or squeezing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients and families are educated about that risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which shaving alternatives are named for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Waxing of the beard area every two weeks under supervision","Weekly application of a topical antibiotic instead of shaving","Chemical depilatories, which inhibit hair growth, and laser-assisted permanent hair removal","Electric razors used daily and always against the direction of the hair growth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — stopping shaving altogether is the first suggestion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the definitive procedure for an abscess that will not drain?","choices":["Aspiration with a fine needle under ultrasound guidance","Observation with warm soaks until it drains on its own","Surgical incision and drainage, covered in detail in a later course","Wide excision of the affected area under general anaesthesia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the aim is to eradicate infection and prevent recurrence. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"Which specimens are taken in serious cellulitis?","choices":["Perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","A potassium hydroxide preparation of the leading edge","Blood cultures and a skin punch biopsy","A superficial swab of the intact affected skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside a complete blood count and creatine phosphokinase. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A patient with cellulitis remains febrile after fifty hours of oral antibiotics. What should happen?","choices":["Continue the same antibiotic, since inflammation takes one to two weeks","Add a topical antibiotic to the affected area alongside the oral","Stop antibiotics entirely and observe for a further forty-eight hours","Change the antimicrobial therapy, guided by culture results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — fever beyond forty-eight hours is the stated trigger. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How does erysipelas present systemically?","choices":["Unrelenting pain out of proportion to examination, with skin colour changing to blue-grey","Sudden onset of malaise, myalgias, chills and high fever within forty-eight hours of skin changes","Gradual onset of low-grade fever and fatigue over the two weeks before the rash appears","No systemic symptoms at all, with the patient consistently afebrile and otherwise well"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with nausea, headache, pruritus, burning, tenderness and swelling. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"What is the pathophysiology of pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["A cut hair curves into the follicular wall and penetrates the skin, causing a foreign body reaction","Bacteria enter the follicle through a break in the skin and produce a follicular pustule","Keratin plugs occlude the pilosebaceous unit and sebum accumulates behind the blockage","Apocrine ducts become obstructed and rupture, seeding inflammation into the dermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It occurs in any shaved area and commonly in Black males with tightly curled facial hair. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Where does acne vulgaris rank among skin diseases in the United States?","choices":["It is the second most common, affecting about 40% of Americans in their lifetime","It is the most common in adolescents only, affecting about 20% of adults","It is uncommon overall, affecting fewer than 10% of Americans in their lifetime","It is the most common skin disease, affecting about 80% of Americans in their lifetime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It usually begins with the onset of puberty. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Can antibiotics prevent acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis after impetigo?","choices":["Only in children over eight, where doxycycline can be used","No, because the immune activation most often precedes antibiotic treatment","Yes, if started within forty-eight hours of the rash appearing","Yes, but only where the organism has first been confirmed on culture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it particularly affects three to seven year olds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How does chronic paronychia present, and over what timescale?","choices":["Oedematous, erythematous, tender nail folds without fluctuance, present at least six weeks","Rapid onset of erythema and swelling two to five days after trauma, often fluctuant","Painless thickening and yellowing of the nail plate over months without inflammation","Grouped painful vesicles on the distal finger appearing over twenty-four hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nail plates thicken and discolour, and cuticles separate from the plate. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"Which factors predispose to hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Hot weather, excessive perspiration, obesity, apocrine duct obstruction, secondary infection and smoking","Cold dry weather, low body weight, occupational chemical exposure and repeated hand immersion","Impaired lymphatic drainage, tinea pedis, obesity and any pre-existing skin infection","Puberty, androgen excess, genetics, lithium therapy and acnegenic mineral oil exposure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is described as essential in management. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is a concern in a drained furuncle?","choices":["Culture the material obtained at incision and drainage","Start linezolid empirically before any material is obtained","Swab the nasal vestibule of every household contact first","Defer drainage until the culture result becomes available"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and give endocarditis prophylaxis to at-risk patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"When are oral antibiotics indicated in acne vulgaris?","choices":["For moderate to severe acne too widespread for topicals, or after an inadequate response over six to eight weeks","For comedonal acne at first presentation, before any topical therapy has been trialled at all","For any patient whose acne has failed to clear completely within two weeks of starting treatment","For patients with acne scarring, in order to prevent any further scars from developing over time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they are prescribed for the shortest time possible, about three to four months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why does devitalised tissue in cellulitis require surgical debridement?","choices":["It is not perfused, so antibiotics cannot reach the site","It harbours resistant organisms that no oral antibiotic will cover","It prevents accurate culture of the organism from the affected area","It causes pain that cannot be controlled with systemic analgesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed or blanched skin is the clue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What kind of organism is Cutibacterium acnes?","choices":["An anaerobic Gram-positive rod that is part of normal skin flora","An aerobic Gram-negative rod acquired from contaminated water","An aerobic Gram-positive coccus carried in the nasal vestibule","A dimorphic yeast that colonises moist intertriginous surfaces"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It was formerly known as Propionibacterium acnes and promotes inflammation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What proportion of erysipelas cases involve the lower extremities?","choices":["About 20%","About 50%","Close to 100%","About 80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the face is the other common site. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which conditions are named in the differential for folliculitis?","choices":["Discoid lupus, deep vein thrombosis and cellulitis","Necrotizing fasciitis, deep vein thrombosis and contact dermatitis","Acne, tinea barbae, molluscum contagiosum and eczema","Thermal burn, allergic contact dermatitis and herpes zoster"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all four can produce papules in a follicular distribution. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which single clue is emphasised for recognising necrotizing fasciitis early?","choices":["Coral-red fluorescence of the affected skin under a Wood's lamp","Unrelenting pain out of proportion to the physical examination","A sharply demarcated plaque raised above the surrounding skin","Honey-coloured crusting over a superficial epidermal erosion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — patients are often diagnosed with cellulitis, sent home, and return worse. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"Which tests may be used in acute paronychia and what does each exclude?","choices":["Nasal swab for staphylococcal carriage, plucked hair for dermatophytes, biopsy for follicular disease","Gram stain and culture for the bacterial cause, potassium hydroxide for candida, Tzanck smear for herpetic whitlow","Wood's lamp for coral-red fluorescence, potassium hydroxide for dermatophytes, biopsy for malignancy","Blood cultures for bacteraemia, creatine phosphokinase for muscle damage, imaging for osteomyelitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is usually a clinical diagnosis, so testing is directed rather than routine. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 118"}]};
